    "notes": "Two sessions October 23-24, 1962 at Plaza Sound Studios, NYC. Track-to-session assignment not documented in Wikipedia \u2014 all personnel play across all tracks. Reissue CDs add alternate takes of Thermo (take 2) and Sweet 'n' Sour (take 4) as bonus tracks. Original 1963 Riverside RM 438. BBC: 'hard bop at its finest.' All Music Guide: Blakey's 'best work.' Engineered by Ray Fowler (not Rudy Van Gelder \u2014 recorded at Plaza Sound, not Van Gelder Studio)."
  },
  "ballot": {
    "tier": "consensus_core",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "This captures an exceptional Jazz Messengers lineup - Blakey, Freddie Hubbard, Wayne Shorter, Curtis Fuller, Cedar Walton, and Reggie Workman - whose session gravity makes it a major document of peak hard bop. The performances combine Blakey's forceful treatment of \"Caravan\" with Shorter's \"Sweet n Sour\" and \"This Is for Albert\" and Hubbard's \"Thermo.\"",
    "case_against": "The album is respected but may be less indispensable than Blakey landmarks such as Moanin, A Night at Birdland, Mosaic, or Free for All. Penguins reported three-star assessment places it below the most elevated Blakey entries.",
    "recommendation": "The council proposes that John approve Caravan as a strong core-period addition.",
